 often combined with the first and 🞄second pers. sg. (± ahám or tváṃ), so'hám, 🞄I as such;
 in Br. it is frequently combined 🞄with the relatives, yáḥ, yádi, and yáthā, 🞄[] 🞄at the beginning of a sentence, and owing to 🞄this common initial usage, is often retained 🞄with relatives either pleonastically or where 🞄the construction requires another gender or 🞄number;
 in the Sāṅkhya sa is used to designate 🞄Puruṣa (like eṣa, ka, and ya).

